 # Thunderbird AI Mail Intelligence
 
-This in-tree component adds a local-first AI monitor for mail. It is disabled
-by default and stores generated data under the Thunderbird profile in `ai/`.
+This component adds one conversational product, **Assistant**, plus local mail
+analysis, retrieval, triage, workflow previews, security integration, data
+governance, and a read-only MCP surface. It is disabled by default and stores
+generated state beneath the Thunderbird profile.
+
+For a user-facing description of the controls and a repeatable end-to-end
+Assistant test, see [User guide and Assistant test](User-Guide-and-AI-Assistant-Test.md).
+For the generated 10K corpus, gold retrieval checks, and the per-message
+processing view, see [Synthetic corpus and processing Timeline](Synthetic-Corpus-and-Processing-Timeline.md).
+For the complete message-to-Assistant architecture, model boundaries, and the
+current evaluation state, see [AI Mail Pipeline and Evaluation Reference](AI-Mail-Pipeline-and-Evaluation.md).
+The dated [2026-09-04 evaluation record](AI-Evaluation-Run-2026-09-04.md)
+contains the exact completed synthetic results and the interrupted matrix-run
+finding without presenting it as a pass.
+
+## Supported runtime
+
+Assistant chat uses a configured HTTP endpoint. Supported saved source types
+are `ollama`, `litellm`, and `openai-compatible`; all use the same source
+selection, request, cancellation, health, and provenance path. Assistant does
+not run local GGUF or ONNX models and Thunderbird does not start or supervise
+an AI helper process.
+
+Thunderbird-owned deterministic work remains available without an endpoint:
+
+- canonical MIME parsing and trust/authentication extraction;
+- Drain3-style structural template mining, template and regex extraction;
+- PII policy and redaction;
+- deterministic category rules with an optional user-trained sparse Naive Bayes routing classifier;
+- lexical, sender, entity, template-family, thread, and scope retrieval;
+- deterministic fallback vectors and compact summaries.
+
+Retired helper, GGUF, ONNX, CPU-local, and GPU-local preferences are migration
+inputs only. They normalize to endpoint or deterministic modes. Migration does
+not delete mailbox data, saved endpoint sources, credentials, or legacy model
+files. Legacy artifacts are removable only through an explicit cleanup action.
+
+## Endpoint configuration
+
+Set `mail.ai.enabled` to `true`, add a source in **Settings > Sources**, select
+it for Assistant, and run **Test Assistant**. Saved source metadata is stored
+in `ai/sources.json`; credentials are stored in Login Manager.
+
+The Sources page tests a source according to its assigned capability: Assistant,
+Background, Analyzer, and Summarizer sources receive a chat probe; embedding-
+only sources such as `bge-m3` receive an embedding probe. Assigning a source to
+Background, or saving it with **Use endpoint for background summaries** enabled,
+also enables the background runtime gate automatically. A source is therefore
+not shown as configured for background work while that gate remains off.
+
