+
+The Assistant also maintains a compact, source-backed local mail graph. Each
+analysed message contributes deterministic nodes for its message, sender,
+sender domain, folder, thread, category, template, extracted entities, actions,
+and risks. Directed edges such as `sent_by`, `in_thread`, `mentions`, and
+`has_action` carry the originating message ID and deterministic-local
+provenance. The graph is updated as individual SQLite rows, so changing one
+message does not rewrite a mailbox-sized graph or JSON document.
+
+When an endpoint Assistant is configured, it may choose four bounded,
+read-only graph tools: a graph overview, an entity neighbourhood lookup, a
+source-linked relationship-path lookup, and repeated-entity topic communities.
+It may also request a bounded document outline for a source message returned
+by local RAG. The outline is derived from local source-text headings and child
+passage offsets; it does not ask a model to manufacture a hierarchy. These
+tools only expose records inside the user-selected scope and return the source
+message IDs needed for citations. Thunderbird treats every returned mail value
+as untrusted data, applies the normal external-redaction policy, and records
+each invocation in the Assistant trace.
+
+The graph remains local-first. **Upgrade with Ollama** now has an optional
+**Enrich graph** check box. When selected, Thunderbird keeps the endpoint
+result as a distinct graph artifact and accepts only model-extracted entity
+values that occur verbatim in Thunderbird's canonical local record. Accepted
+edges are marked `endpoint-enriched`, include endpoint source/model/time and a
+lower confidence, and never replace deterministic edges or Thunderbird-owned
+local fields.
